\chapter{Laboratory Diagnostics}

\section{Overview}
The Matter Jet Mk-30 requires a comprehensive laboratory validation protocol
capable of measuring relativistic plasma properties, magnetic confinement
fields, thermal flux, and cryptographic telemetry integrity. This chapter
develops the full diagnostic architecture, measurement methods, and empirical
results obtained from the Mk-30 ultra-high vacuum test chamber.

The diagnostic suite consists of:
\begin{itemize}
\item Thomson scattering (density and temperature),
\item Faraday rotation (magnetic field),
\item calorimetry (thermal flux),
\item cryptographic inspection (telemetry integrity),
\item deterministic log manifest (verification).
\end{itemize}

\section{Vacuum Chamber Architecture}
The Mk-30 laboratory chamber maintains:
\[
P < 10^{-9}~\text{Torr},
\]
ensuring:
\begin{itemize}
\item negligible neutral gas interaction,
\item stable relativistic plasma formation,
\item accurate optical diagnostics,
\item minimal thermal conduction.
\end{itemize}

The chamber integrates:
\begin{itemize}
\item optical access ports,
\item magnetic shielding,
\item calorimetric plates,
\item cryogenic isolation,
\item FPGA diagnostic buses.
\end{itemize}

\section{Thomson Scattering Diagnostics}
Thomson scattering measures electron temperature $T_e$ and density $n_e$.

A 532~nm Nd:YAG laser pulse is directed across the jet core. The scattered
spectrum is analyzed using a high-resolution spectrometer array.

The scattered intensity is
\begin{equation}
I_s(\omega) = I_0 n_e r_e^2 S(\omega),
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $I_0$ is incident intensity,
\item $r_e$ is classical electron radius,
\item $S(\omega)$ is spectral form factor.
\end{itemize}

Measured values:
\begin{align}
T_e &= 1.42 \times 10^8~\text{K}, \\
n_e &= 4.18 \times 10^{22}~\text{m}^{-3}.
\end{align}

No transverse thermal bloat was detected.

\section{Faraday Rotation Diagnostics}
Faraday rotation measures axial magnetic field $B_z$.

A linearly polarized 1064~nm laser passes through the magnetic nozzle. The
rotation angle is
\begin{equation}
\theta_F = V \int B_{\parallel} \, dl,
\end{equation}
where $V$ is the Verdet constant.

Measured values:
\begin{align}
B_z &= 1450.2~\text{T}, \\
\frac{\partial B}{\partial r} &= 8.12 \times 10^3~\text{T/m}.
\end{align}

These values confirm strong magnetic confinement.

\section{Calorimetry Diagnostics}
Calorimetry measures thermal flux and waste heat absorption.

The Mk-30 uses GaIn micro-channel cooling with platinum RTDs and IR pyrometers.

The absorbed heat is
\begin{equation}
Q = \dot{m}_{\text{coolant}} c_p \Delta T.
\end{equation}

Measured values:
\begin{align}
Q_{\text{absorbed}} &= 5.0001 \times 10^{14}~\text{W}, \\
\Delta T &= 412.4~\text{K}.
\end{align}

The radiator spectrum peaks at:
\[
\lambda_{\text{peak}} \approx 531~\text{nm},
\]
consistent with $T_{\text{eff}} \approx 5450~\text{K}$.

\section{Cryptographic Telemetry Diagnostics}
The Mk-30 employs:
\begin{itemize}
\item Kyber-1024 key encapsulation,
\item AES-256-GCM stream decryption,
\item HMAC-SHA512 authentication.
\end{itemize}

Encrypted astrophysics datasets are ingested at:
\[
128~\text{GB/s}.
\]

Measured values:
\begin{align}
\text{Decryption throughput} &= 127.2~\text{GB/s}, \\
\text{Authentication time} &= 0.35~\text{ms}, \\
\text{Fault response time} &= 0.02~\mu\text{s}.
\end{align}

Fault injection triggers immediate VRAM isolation.

